## ⛓️ How a Human Hunter Chains Bugs Together
To understand how human intuition beats automated scanners, you have to look at Exploit Chaining. AI scanners look at vulnerabilities in isolation. If a bug is low-risk, the AI flags it as a low priority and moves on.
A human hunter looks at three "low-risk" bugs and treats them like a puzzle, gluing them together to create a critical-severity exploit that pays a massive bounty.
Here is a real-world scenario of a human chaining three low-risk flaws to achieve full account takeover:
## 🧩 Bug 1: Self-XSS (Severity: Low/Informational)

* The Flaw: A tester finds that the "Update Profile Nickname" field allows JavaScript injection.
* Why AI stops: The AI flags this as Self-XSS because the script only runs in the tester’s own browser when they look at their own profile. Since you cannot force another user to look at your personal settings dashboard, the AI closes the ticket as harmless.

## 🧩 Bug 2: Lack of CSRF Protection on a Content Widget (Severity: Low)

* The Flaw: The website has a public widget feature (like an interactive bio wall) where users can invite others to view their public profile. This invitation request lacks an anti-CSRF token.
* Why AI stops: The AI sees that an attacker can trick a victim into sending a blank profile invitation. It classifies it as a low-severity nuisance bug since no sensitive data is leaked or changed.

## 🧩 Bug 3: DOM-Based Open Redirect (Severity: Low)

* The Flaw: The login page uses a parameter to remember where a user was going before they logged in (e.g., login.php?next=/dashboard). The tester finds that if they change /dashboard to an external website, the page still redirects there.
* Why AI stops: An open redirect just sends a user to a different website. On its own, it doesn’t steal data.

