## 🤖 How AI Scanners Are Configured to Find Memory Flaws
While humans excel at the creative logic above, AI dominates when auditing source code for brutal, deep-seated memory corruption. Security teams configure AI static analysis tools (SAST) and machine learning models to map out Control Flow Graphs (CFGs).
Here is how an AI model is mathematically trained to catch a Use-After-Free (UAF) memory leak in C++ code:

[Memory Allocation] ──► [Pointer Created] ──► [Memory Freed/Cleaned]
                                                      │
                                                      ▼
                                       AI flags path if Pointer is accessed here!


   1. Taint Analysis (Tracing the Variable): The AI tracks every single variable from the moment it is initialized (the "source") to the moment it leaves the program execution (the "sink").
   2. State Machine Modeling: The AI tracks the lifetime state of a memory address. It creates strict mathematical rules:
   * State 0: Unallocated
      * State 1: Allocated
      * State 2: Deallocated (Freed)
   3. Pattern Matching: If the AI traces a path through the code graph where a pointer is referenced while the memory address is in State 2, it instantly triggers an alert. A human developer reading a 5-million-line codebase could easily miss a pointer being reuse-called 2,000 lines after it was cleared, but the AI's graph tracking never blinks.

